  "abstract": "<jats:title>Abstract</jats:title>\n                  <jats:p>Inertial measurement unit (IMU) is widely used in navigation and motion sensing due to their small size, low cost, and high sampling rate. However, low-cost micro-electro-mechanical system (MEMS) IMU suffers from significant measurement noise, which severely affects navigation accuracy. To address this issue, this paper proposes a self-supervised IMU denoising method that integrates temporal decomposition with a multi-module fusion network. The approach employs dilated causal convolution to capture short-term local dependencies while avoiding future information leakage. A multi-head attention mechanism is incorporated to enhance long-range dependency modeling, and an long short term memory (LSTM) module is introduced to improve temporal sequence learning. Furthermore, a temporal decomposition strategy is designed to separate high- and low-frequency components, allowing targeted denoising while preserving essential motion information. Experimental results on real world datasets demonstrate that the proposed method outperforms traditional autoencoder (AE), convolutional AE, and LSTM models in terms of mean absolute error, root mean square error, and maximum absolute error, achieving more robust denoising performance in complex environments.</jats:p>",
